## Getting something working is different from understanding it

By the end of the first stage, I had a working elevator simulation.

That was useful, but it wasn't enough.

Once the code existed, I needed to be able to look at it and understand what was happening.

I didn't want the project to become a black box where I could describe what I wanted and ask an AI to keep changing things for me.

The whole point of the Playground is experimentation. To experiment properly, I need to understand the system I'm experimenting on.

So this stage became less about adding features and more about turning the implementation into something I could reason about.

---

## Building a mental model

The first thing I did was go through the project systematically rather than reading files randomly.

This gave me a way to follow the system from the smallest pieces upward.

I started with the configuration and state definitions because they describe the vocabulary of the simulation.

Then came the elevator itself: its position, velocity, movement phases, doors, and passengers.

After that came the objects interacting with the elevator, followed by the systems that create requests and decide where elevators should go.

Finally, I looked at the simulation that coordinates everything and the renderer that displays it.

The important part wasn't memorizing every function.

It was understanding the flow of information.

A passenger creates a request.

The dispatcher decides what should handle it.

The simulation applies that decision.

The elevator physically moves.

Events and statistics record what happened.

The renderer observes the resulting state.

Once I could follow that chain, the project stopped feeling like a collection of Python files and started feeling like one system.

---

## Separating physical reality from decisions

One of the biggest things I gained from this process was a clearer distinction between three different kinds of logic:

What physically happens.

An elevator has a position and velocity. It accelerates and decelerates. Its doors have states. Passengers occupy space inside it.

What decisions are made.

Which elevator should receive a request? Which stop should be served next? Should a request wait?

What coordinates the experiment.

When does time advance? When are passengers generated? When are events processed? When are statistics updated?

These became roughly:
mechanics/
    What exists and how it behaves

settings_configs/
    What can be configured or swapped

simulation/
    How the experiment runs


That distinction was useful because it gave me a way to reason about future changes.

If I want to change the physics, I should not need to rewrite the dispatcher.

If I want to test another dispatcher, I should not need to change the elevator's movement model.

If I want to render the simulation differently, the simulation itself should not care.

That separation wasn't something I wanted simply because it is "clean code."

I wanted it because it makes experiments easier.

---

## Restructuring the project

The original implementation worked, but its structure wasn't quite how I wanted to think about the system.

I reorganized it around the responsibilities I had identified during the specification stage.

I also consolidated related definitions where doing so made the system easier to navigate.

This wasn't a large rewrite for the sake of rewriting.

It was mostly about making the structure match the mental model I had developed while studying the code.

---

## Understanding before changing

One thing I deliberately tried not to do was change code simply because I would have written it differently.

That distinction became important with the elevator physics.

I went through the movement and braking logic closely enough to understand what it was doing.

The kinematics were not necessarily written in the same style I would have chosen, but I had no reason to replace them.

The same applied to things like braking distance and door timing.

Understanding code does not mean rewriting everything until it looks like my code.

Sometimes the correct conclusion is:

> I understand this, and I don't need to change it.

That was actually useful for me because it forced me to separate preference from problem.

If something is wrong, I should fix it.

If something is unfamiliar but correct, I should understand it.

Those are different situations.

---

## Small things that surfaced during the review

Once I started reading the implementation carefully, I also found smaller issues that would have been easy to miss if I had only cared about whether the program ran.

None of them were dramatic bugs.

But they were useful because they showed the difference between:

> "The program runs."

and:

> "I understand what every line is actually doing."

I documented these rather than quietly removing every imperfection during restructuring.

The point of this playground wasn't to make every project look untouched or perfectly polished.

It was to make the state of the experiment honest.

---

## Putting everything into one place

Another small architectural decision came from thinking ahead to experimentation.

I wanted to be able to compare different versions of the simulation without having to reconstruct the entire system every time.

That influenced how I grouped the simulation implementations and dispatchers.

If I eventually have:

BaselineDispatcher
CapacityAwareDispatcher
RLDispatcher

I want them to share the same underlying world.

Otherwise, a performance difference could come from differences in the simulation rather than differences in decision making.

This is the beginning of a control group mindset that becomes much more important later.

The baseline isn't something I want to throw away as soon as I have a "better" idea.

It is something I want to keep healthy enough to compare against.

---

## What I did/didn't change

The restructuring wasn't about claiming ownership over every line.

It was about making the system understandable enough for me to take responsibility for the decisions being made.

I changed the organization where the structure didn't match the way I understood the system.

I cleaned up small inconsistencies.

I removed or rewrote comments once I understood what the code was actually doing.

But I deliberately left working pieces alone when I couldn't justify changing their behavior.

That distinction matters to the way I'm approaching AI assisted development.

The goal isn't:

> "AI wrote it, so I need to rewrite it."

Nor is it:

> "It works, so I don't need to understand it."

It's somewhere in between:

> Use the acceleration, then do the intellectual work required to understand and control what was produced.

---

## The role of AI changed here

The first stage used AI mostly as an implementation accelerator.

This stage used it differently.

I was asking questions about the code, tracing behavior, checking assumptions, and investigating parts of the architecture that I didn't immediately understand.

That meant the workflow became much more iterative:

design
   ↓
implementation
   ↓
study
   ↓
run
   ↓
question
   ↓
change
   ↓
run again

AI helped me move through that loop much faster, but it didn't remove the need to understand the result.

In fact, having the code appear quickly made that understanding more important.

The faster I could produce an implementation, the faster I could get to the interesting part:

Does this actually represent the system I had in my head?

That was important because my next idea was no longer just about making the elevator move differently.

I wanted to change how it served multiple passengers.

And that exposed something I hadn't fully appreciated yet:

sometimes the problem you think you're solving lives in one component, but the architecture underneath it assumes something completely different.

